messages.text:
Status update:

- **#9817 landed** (held shared allocations stay revivable). **#9824** (store caches) is ready, stacked on it. It passed workspace check, the authority gate, store lib tests (1083) and clippy, after a merge with current master.
- **Semantic lane:** all checks passed:
  - authority gate;
  - semantic lib (188 tests);
  - issue_2774;
  - JS/TS semantic service;
  - clippy;
  - pack drift check.

  Review then found one real gap. A storeless `WorkspaceAnalyzer::Empty` panicked when building its semantic oracle, because there is no owner budget to ask for. I added `RetentionBudget::retain_nothing()`, a zero-capacity budget that refuses everything. Storeless workspaces use it, so they compute per request and retain nothing. A hard-won regression test covers it, and it is back in review.
- **Analysis lane:** all checks passed:
  - authority gate;
  - analysis lib (900 tests);
  - semantic-model conformance;
  - clippy.

  It goes to review once the semantic review is done; I can only run one review at a time.
- **M3 L1 committed** (27b563c9e1). One engine per root now lives in the snapshot caches, demands run under a scoped read lease, and the authority is payload-free. Before committing, I made two fixes myself:
  - the thread-local host accessors are now closure-scoped, so references cannot escape a demand;
  - store errors in a demand now return an unavailable answer instead of being swallowed.

  The integration test shows parent reuse through child identity across fresh readers.
- **Rust statics:** chunk C1 is committed (handle types, `_with` helper variants, adapter wiring). C2 (imports, declarations, graph support, hierarchy) is running.

The analysis lane fix is committed (02d9b87678) and back in review. The selected semantic models and their overlay now take required charges, so they are always published, and budget pressure can no longer change what the analyzer answers.

My first version of `acquire_required` evicted nothing when the request was larger than the whole budget. The new unit test caught that. The fix evicts as much as can be freed before charging. After it:

- the core budget tests pass (21);
- the analysis lib tests pass (900);
- the semantic-model suite passes (192);
- the workspace check and clippy are clean.

Rust statics C2 is still finishing. The parallel items from earlier (M4 route map, V2, index-answer table, L2 replay, reconciliation eviction) are still waiting for your go-ahead.

Status update:

- **#9838 has landed** (analysis caches). Of M1, only the Rust caches lane is still open.
- **C3 hid a regression, which I fixed.** The C3 child changed a hard-won test so that, after an update, it expected the *stale* crate name ("before" instead of "after"). The cause was my own earlier ruling: I said the naming statics were cleared only at analyzer construction, but `RustAnalyzer::update` cleared them too. A `Cargo.toml` edit can rename a crate without changing any Rust source. The fix:
  - `RustCrateNamingCaches::invalidate_all()` is now called at both update points;
  - the test keeps its original expectation, and passes;
  - C3 is committed (beb35b68f9);
  - I corrected the ruling file and saved a memory: audit every changed test expectation in child diffs.
- **C4 is running.** It moves the last callers (store, analysis, the rest), deletes the six statics and the old wrappers, and merges the two near-identical cache helpers into one. Its brief says a test's expected value may never change. After C4 comes the Rust lane PR.

The parallel M3/M4 items are still waiting for your go-ahead.

